  "interpretation": "Sample verified: two terminal fresh-rootfs read-only unidbg runs, eight fixed-input calls. The +0x243dac helper receives 263 data bytes, a 20-byte key and zero drop. Existing Python vm9_stream_cipher independently transforms those inputs; all output bytes equal native RC4 and independent ARC4. The actual configuration byte stores read all 263 bytes in reverse address order with no gaps. The XOR operand is a reduction of twelve observed leaf bytes, starting with a captured zero operand written at +0x16db74/cursor +0x9af00. The retained reverse_xor_configuration model produces the actual 263-byte Protobuf input. Python then unpacks ITS OWN generated payload with existing ELF descriptors, selects the actual field, decodes the material and supplies its halves to independent Java AES. Actual native ciphertext and the complete prior field/constructor/writer/JNI, preimage/SHA-256, AES, seed, layout, pre-block, XTEA and material provenance ledgers all remain exact. No-reversal, no-XOR and wrong-mask candidates fail per call. Global clone discovery observed that the reverse source is a clone of RC4 output; old matching addresses from prior calls alone are not current ownership proof. The paired stream proof compares bytes from the actual current helper call directly. The first 240-second model run timed out late in call four and is excluded; final paired runs use a bounded 420-second deadline. RC4 input/key, reduction leaves and lookup key remain explicit runtime observations, not independently generated values. No raw or encoded operands, material, keys, IVs, pointers, scalar masks or operand digests are published. Earlier decryption, other inputs, Android equivalence, the full independent signer and ordinary Cronet six-header online acceptance remain unverified."
